1. what is a tree data structure ?
Solution: a tree is a non-linear data structure used to store data in a hierarchical manner. 
think about a family tree or the folder structure on your computer.

For example:

                a 
               / \
              b   c
            /  \  
            d   e
              / / \
              f g h 

consider this tree:

                          10
                         /  \
                        20  30
                       / \   /
                      40 50  60

here, 

10 is the root node
20 and 30 are children of 10
10 is the parent of 20 and 30
40, 50 and 60 are leaf nodes
the connection between two nodes is called an edge.

2. important tree terminologies 

term                              meaning 
root                              topmost node of a tree
parent                            node having children 
child                             node connected below another node
leaf                              node having no children 
edge                              connection between two nodes
sibling                           nodes having same parent 
subtree                           smaller tree inside a tree
depth                             distance from root to node
height                            longest path from node to leaf 

VERY IMPORTANT TO NOTE that -->

height is longest path from node to leaf whereas depth is distance from root to node.

For example:

                     a
                    / \
                   b   c
                  / \ 
                 d   e

root=a
children of a = b,c
parent of d = b
siblings 1st pair = b and c
siblings 2nd pair = d and e
leaf nodes = c,d,e 

leaf nodes are those nodes which are having no children.

3. implementing a tree in python

first, understand how to create a Node.

a node generally contains two things.
1. data
2. reference to its children.

Step 1. create a node class

Code:

class Node:
      def __init__(self, data):
              self.data=data
              self.left=None
              self.right=None

Explanation of this code is as following:

class Node: Creates a Node Class
self.data:  stores the value
self.left: Stores the reference to the left child
self.right: stores the reference to the right child
None: Means there is no child currently 

Step 2: Create tree nodes 

Code:

class Node:
    def __init__(self, data):
          self.data=data
          self.left=None
          self.right=None
root=Node(10)
root.left=Node(20)
root.right=Node(30)
root.left.left=Node(40)
root.left.right=Node(50)

Our tree looks like this :

                   10
                  / \
                 20  30
                / \
              40  50





